# How this course was made (W3C PROV-O): written by AI agents, then checked
# in the rounds below, against the sources below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-28T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://archive.org/details/Fsi-KoreanBasicCourse-StudentText> ,
              <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://en.wiktionary.org/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ,
                       <#review-5> ,
                       <#review-6> ,
                       <#review-7> ,
                       <#review-8> ,
                       <#review-9> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "The course was planned and written by AI agents working for Anton Wiklund, from a concept for all ten chapters (issue #132), and checked by AI agents and by machines (the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators, its course and Markdown checks, and a second SHACL validator); no person has reviewed it."@en ,
                 "Selection: one topic, food and eating together, told as one story with the same people, so that dishes, spice, portions, paying and thanks come back from chapter to chapter. The ten chapters of the concept: a first meeting at a café, deciding what to eat, a meal at a small 찌개 restaurant, who pays, delivery by phone, a market run, cooking together, where the two friends agree to speak 반말, dinner at Minji's family, a first team dinner (회식) and, months later, their regular place, where the speech level changes with whom Alex talks to; the course ends with the casual 밥 먹었어?. Version 1 had chapters 0 to 2; version 2 adds chapters 3 to 9. The people and places are invented. The level is after Hangul and roughly Basic Korean, about TOPIK I. No card front repeats one of Basic Korean, Everyday Korean phrases or the Korean word decks; the dialogues use some of their phrases, and the theory says where they were learned."@en ,
                 "Structure: 10 chapters of 30 steps in all, three per chapter: the scene, a dialogue with its key phrases explained; how it works, the chapter's grammar point; and another way it goes, a variant dialogue and a note on culture. Each step is read in two chunks and checks two or three questions met in its theory, and each chapter has two review questions, asked only in its final review: 65 step questions and 20 review questions, 85 questions in all. Every question has one correct option (the card's back) and three wrong options (its distractors), each with a note on why it is wrong. The front is Korean in Hangul, in today's standard spelling and spacing and without romanization: 해요체 for what the learner says, 합쇼체 where a real speaker uses it (staff, set phrases, seniors at work), honorific words to and about elders, and 반말 only between Alex and Minji from chapter 6, where some cards test which level fits as well as what the phrase means; lines the learner only hears are marked so in the notes. The back, note and options are in English, Swedish, German, Spanish and French, with du, tu and tú between the two friends and Sie, usted and vous to and from staff, elders and seniors. The theory is in English only."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the course is CC0 1.0. Its dialogues, theory, questions, options and notes are the course's own text, written with the help of AI. The FSI course (a work of the United States Government) and Wikidata (CC0 1.0) allow any reuse, and English Wiktionary (CC BY-SA 4.0) was used to check meanings only; no text was taken from any of the three."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Concept: Korean (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The Korean of the course's concept, with two other concepts it was chosen from: cast, speech levels, the planned card fronts of all ten chapters and the sample step and cards, for correctness, naturalness, standard spelling, overlap with Basic Korean and Everyday Korean phrases, and the release's Markdown rules. Done by an AI agent working independently of the compiling agent; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Applied before the chapters were written: the learner's character is called 알렉스, which names no gender, instead of a name Koreans would hear as slang for teacher; the staff's line 따뜻한 걸로 드릴까요, 아이스로 드릴까요? is carded only as its first half; a guest speaks honorific 해요체 rather than 합쇼체 to a friend's parents, with 합쇼체 kept for set lines; and every dialogue line ends in a hard break before its gloss."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapters: Korean, answers and course rules (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Each chapter as written, reviewed by three AI agents working apart: one for the Korean (grammar, spelling and spacing, naturalness, the speech level each speaker would use, and the glosses and grammar in the theory), one for the backs, notes, wrong options and their notes in English, Swedish, German, Spanish and French (meaning, agreement between the languages, and wrong options that are plausible and do not give the answer away by their form), and one for the course's rules, its Markdown and its questions as flip cards studied later out of context; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 37 findings (18, 10 and 9 in the three chapters), all applied; where two findings proposed different wordings, one was chosen. Among them: the rule for -(으)ㄹ까요 after a stem in ㄹ, -시- explained as honoring the person who acts, 씨 limited to adults on an equal footing, a phrase checked by a step but not met in its theory, wrong options and backs reworded in Swedish, German and Spanish, and notes that now name the honorific 드시다."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Course as a whole (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The three chapters read together by an AI agent for the story's continuity, names and speech levels used the same way throughout, facts taught twice, correct options that stand out by their length or form, fronts repeated within the course or from Basic Korean and Everyday Korean phrases, glosses that disagree with the backs, and the counts in the description; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: two jumps in the story's time were removed, a fact about where a restaurant keeps its spoons was corrected, seven backs or wrong options that stood out by their length were reworded, staff speaking to two customers now use the plural in Swedish and Spanish, and one note's quotation marks were made consistent; the 28 fronts are all different and none repeats one of the other Korean decks."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Sources (AI and machine)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The course's key words against the three sources, searched by an AI agent with scripts: 38 words of the fronts in English Wiktionary's Korean entries, the same words and the dishes in Wikidata through the Wikibase API, and the grammar points (counters, -(으)ㄹ까요?, 못, the honorific -시-, the polite speech styles) in the OCR text of the FSI course. Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No source contradicts a card. Wiktionary has Korean entries for 32 of the 38 words, among them 연하다 (of a liquid: thin, dilute), 동갑 (age-mate), 맛집, 공깃밥 and 드시다 (honorific of 먹다); 년생, 진동벨, 아무거나, 셀프, 인분 and 앞접시 have none, though 생, 아무것 and 접시 do. Wikidata has lexemes for 6 of the words (고기, 못, 안, 끓다, 분, 씨) and items for 비빔밥, 김치찌개, 찌개, 반찬 and caffè americano. The FSI course has 잔 as the counter for cups and glasses, -(으)ㄹ까요?, 못, -(으)시- and its formal and informal polite speech, which are 합쇼체 and 해요체."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The whole release, with the library checker (npm run library), which validates it against Solid Memo's shapes (library deck format 6, card format 5, chapter, step and distractor format 1), DCAT-AP and SKOS, with the reference data, checks the course's outline by its rules and its Markdown by the rules for a release, and with a second, independent SHACL validator (pySHACL, npm run crosscheck) over the same shapes."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The release conforms: the library checker found the library valid with this course in it, and pySHACL found all 234 documents it checks conform."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-28T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 2: concept (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The concept for chapters 3 to 9, written by an AI agent from issue #132 and version 1 (settings, cast, speech levels, grammar points, culture notes and every planned card front), reviewed by another AI agent working independently for its Korean, the speech level each speaker would use, the honorifics, the etiquette of a family dinner and a 회식, overlap with the other Korean decks, fronts that are ambiguous as flip cards and continuity with version 1; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 14 findings, 13 applied to the concept before the chapters were written, among them: -(으)ㄹ게요 is never used in a question; staff on the phone answer 네, 말씀하세요; the younger person invites an older one to drop polite speech with 말 편하게 하세요, while the older one suggests it with 말 놓을까요?; an elder does not answer a young guest with 저도요; the year-end dinner is the first 회식 of someone who started work in December; and two backs no longer give what only the scene says. The 57 planned fronts were all different and none repeated another Korean deck."@en .

<#review-6>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-28T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 2: chapters: Korean, answers and course rules (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Each new chapter as written, reviewed by two AI agents working apart from the writer and from each other: one for the Korean (grammar, spelling and spacing, naturalness, the speech level each speaker would use, the glosses, and the grammar and culture facts in the theory), one for the backs, notes, wrong options and their notes in English, Swedish, German, Spanish and French, the course's rules, its Markdown and its questions as flip cards studied later out of context; a third AI agent applied the findings. Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 118 findings (18, 17, 12, 18, 14, 18 and 21 in chapters 3 to 9), 111 applied; the other 7 repeated or contradicted another finding on the same text, and one of the two was applied. Among them: the standard spelling 소짜, the Korean quoted in notes made more natural, the Onnuri gift certificates described as accepted at registered stalls and shops, how 더치페이 came about, and wrong options and backs reworded in Swedish, German and Spanish."@en .

<#review-7>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-28T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 2: course as a whole (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The ten chapters read together by an AI agent for the story's continuity and time line, names and speech levels used the same way throughout, facts taught twice, correct options that stand out by their length or form, fronts repeated within the course or from the other Korean decks, glosses that disagree with the backs and notes that point to the wrong chapter; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 11 references to chapters by number were replaced by the scene they name, since the app counts chapters from 1; a phrase quoted from an earlier chapter was corrected, one vegetable is called green onions throughout, a fact chapter 2 teaches is recalled rather than taught again in chapter 3, six phrases quoted from Everyday Korean phrases now name it, one correct option no longer stands out by its form, and ten notes no longer put a period after a Korean question. Nothing in chapters 0 to 2 contradicts the new chapters; the 85 fronts are all different and none repeats one of the other Korean decks."@en .

<#review-8>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-28T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 2: sources (AI and machine)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The new chapters' key words against the three sources, searched by an AI agent with scripts on 28 October 2026: 74 words in English Wiktionary's Korean entries, the same words as Korean lexemes and the dishes and drinks as items in Wikidata through the Wikibase API, and the grammar points and words in the OCR text of both volumes of the FSI course. Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No source contradicts a card. Wiktionary has Korean entries for 69 of the 74 words, among them 진지, 연세 and 주무시다 as the honorific words for rice, age and sleep, 수저, 간, 회식 (company dinner) and 서비스 (on the house); 결제, 결제하다, 조심히, 위하여 and -어치 have none. Wikidata has no new word as a lexeme in the card's sense, and items for the dishes and drinks, among them 짜장면, 짬뽕, 탕수육, 떡볶이, 소주 and 회식. The FSI course has the plain speech made by dropping 요, the honorific stems 주무시- and 계시-, 드리다 as the humble give, and 개 and 잔 as counters; its 1968 rule that a foreigner always speaks politely is dated, and chapter 6 follows how age-mates who become friends speak today."@en .

<#review-9>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-28T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 2: machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The whole release, with the library checker (npm run library), which validates it against Solid Memo's shapes (library deck format 6, card format 5, chapter, step and distractor format 1), DCAT-AP and SKOS, with the reference data, checks the course's outline by its rules and its Markdown by the rules for a release, and with a second, independent SHACL validator (pySHACL, npm run crosscheck) over the same shapes."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The release conforms: the library checker found the library valid with this version in it, and pySHACL found all 279 documents it checks conform."@en .

<https://archive.org/details/Fsi-KoreanBasicCourse-StudentText>
    dcterms:title "Korean Basic Course, Volume 1 (Foreign Service Institute, 1968), as scanned on the Internet Archive" ;
    dcterms:creator "B. Nam Park; edited by Augustus A. Koski; Foreign Service Institute, U.S. Department of State" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Checking: the Internet Archive's OCR text of Volume 1 (romanized Korean and English), and Volume 2 in the same item for its English, searched for the course's grammar points and words. Volume 1 explains 잔 as the counter for \"cups or glasses of liquid\" (\"a cup of coffee\"), -(으)ㄹ까요?, 못, the honorific suffix -(으)시- and its formal and informal polite speech, and has 고기 (meat), 맵다 and 김치. A work of the United States Government: the title page reads \"B. Nam Park … Foreign Service Institute, Washington, D.C. 1968\" and \"For sale by the Superintendent of Documents, U.S. Government Printing Office\". 17 U.S.C. § 105: \"Copyright protection under this title is not available for any work of the United States Government\". Section 105 applies in the United States; what the course takes from it is checking of grammar points and meanings, not its text. For version 2 (28 October 2026), both volumes' OCR text were searched again: Volume 1 explains the plain speech made by dropping 요 and the honorific stems such as 주무시- and 계시-, and has 개 as the counter for objects, 잔 for glasses, 드리다 as the humble give, and 시장, 주소, 전화번호, 칼 and 간장; Volume 2's units on an invitation have hosts playing down their food and guests thanking for the meal."@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata (Korean lexemes and items)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors (Wikimedia Foundation)" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Checking: Korean lexemes and items queried for the course's key words and dishes on 16 October 2026. Wikidata has few Korean lexemes: 6 of the 38 words have one (고기, 못, 안, 끓다, 분, 씨), and the dishes have items (비빔밥 Q648352, 김치찌개 Q701756, 찌개 Q1062483, 반찬 Q716245, caffè americano Q1152551); 공깃밥 has none. https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing — \"All structured data (i.e. the main, Property, Lexeme, and EntitySchema namespaces) is released into the public domain under Creative Commons Zero.\" For version 2 (28 October 2026): none of the new key words has a lexeme in the card's sense (개 has only the animal, 불 only the prefix), and the dishes and drinks have items, among them 짜장면 Q1141033, 짬뽕 Q17063016, 탕수육 Q29515244, 떡볶이 Q1190475, 어묵 Q594675, 소주 Q696105 and 회식 Q55182423."@en .

<https://en.wiktionary.org/>
    dcterms:title "English Wiktionary (Korean entries)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wiktionary contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Verification only; no text was taken from it. 38 key words of the fronts were looked up on 16 October 2026: 32 have a Korean entry, and the definitions read agree with the cards (방금 just now, 연하다 of a liquid thin or dilute, 진하다 strong, 동갑 age-mate, 맛집 delicious restaurant, 공깃밥 rice in a bowl, 반찬 side dishes, 드시다 honorific to eat or drink, 맵다 spicy, 끓다 to boil, 잔 a glass of a beverage). 년생, 진동벨, 아무거나, 셀프, 인분 and 앞접시 have no entry. Text is available under CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Wiktionary:Copyrights). For version 2, 74 key words of the new fronts were looked up on 28 October 2026: 69 have a Korean entry, and the definitions read agree with the cards (진지 honorific of 밥, 연세 honorific age, 주무시다 honorific of 자다, 수저 spoon and chopsticks, 간 salty seasoning, 회식 company dinner, 서비스 on the house, 시키다 to order food); 결제, 결제하다, 조심히, 위하여 and -어치 have no entry."@en .

