                              "Ce serait 마실 건 제가 살게요 ; 잘 마실게요 remercie celui qui paie."@fr .

<#ch-paying-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-paying> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Splitting the bill

*Another way it goes: at the counter Minji offers again, but this time Alex won't hear of it.*

**민지:** 오늘은 제가 살게요.\\
*It's my treat today.*

**알렉스:** 아니에요. 오늘은 우리 나눠서 내요.\\
*No, really. Let's split the bill today.*

**민지:** 그래요. 그럼 제가 먼저 계산할게요.\\
*All right. Then I'll pay first, and we'll settle up after.*

*Minji pays the 23,000 won by card. Outside, Alex takes out a phone.*

**알렉스:** 얼마 보내면 돼요?\\
*How much should I send you?*

**민지:** 만 천오백 원이요.\\
*11,500 won.*

**알렉스:** 지금 보낼게요. … 보냈어요!\\
*I'll send it now. … Done!*

**민지:** 네, 받았어요. 고마워요.\\
*Yes, got it. Thanks.*

---

**우리 나눠서 내요**: "Let's split the bill." 나누다 is to divide, and -아/어서 links it to the next verb: divide, then pay. With 우리 (we), the 해요체 present makes a suggestion, as 가요! did when they set off for dinner.

**제가 먼저 계산할게요**: "I'll pay first, and we'll settle up after." 먼저 is first: one person pays the whole bill at the counter, and the others pay their share back.

**얼마 보내면 돼요?**: "How much should I send you?" 보내다 is to send, here money from a phone, and -(으)면 돼요? asks what will do: literally "if I send how much, is that fine?" 만 천오백 원 is 11,500 won. Korean counts in ten thousands (만), so 11,500 is "ten thousand, one thousand, five hundred", and 23,000 is 이만 삼천.

**Culture note:** There is no tipping in Korea, and, as at the 찌개 restaurant, you usually pay at the counter on the way out. Whoever invited, or the older or more senior person, often pays for everyone, and a treat is usually returned: with the coffee straight after, or with the next meal. Among friends and young people, splitting is common too. It is called 더치페이 (a Korean coinage from the English "Dutch treat", going Dutch) or N분의 1 ("one nth", read 엔분의 일). Often one person pays it all and the others send their share at once by a banking app or a payment app such as KakaoPay or Toss, or the payer sends them a payment request. Many restaurants will also split the bill at the counter if you ask with 따로 계산할게요, which you know from Everyday Korean phrases."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-paying-3a> ,
                         <#q-paying-3b> .

<#q-paying-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-28T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "우리 나눠서 내요"@ko ;
    solid-memo:back "Let's split the bill"@en ,
                    "Vi delar på notan"@sv ,
                    "Lass uns die Rechnung teilen"@de ,
                    "Paguemos a medias"@es ,
                    "On partage l'addition"@fr ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Said in polite 해요체 to a friend or acquaintance at the end of a meal, when you want to share the cost instead of being treated. 나누다 means to divide, and -아/어서 links it to 내다, to pay; with 우리 (we), the 해요체 present is a suggestion. The usual reply is 그래요 or 좋아요, and often one person then pays and the other sends their share."@en ,
                        "Sägs i artig 해요체 till en vän eller bekant i slutet av en måltid, när man vill dela på kostnaden i stället för att bli bjuden. 나누다 betyder dela, och -아/어서 knyter det till 내다, betala; med 우리 (vi) blir presens i 해요체 ett förslag. Det vanliga svaret är 그래요 eller 좋아요, och ofta betalar sedan en och den andra skickar sin del."@sv ,
                        "Sagt man im höflichen 해요체 am Ende eines Essens zu Freunden oder Bekannten, wenn man sich die Kosten teilen will, statt eingeladen zu werden. 나누다 heißt teilen, und -아/어서 verbindet es mit 내다, zahlen; mit 우리 (wir) wird das Präsens im 해요체 zum Vorschlag. Die übliche Antwort ist 그래요 oder 좋아요, und oft zahlt dann einer, und der andere überweist seinen Teil."@de ,
                        "Se dice en el estilo cortés 해요체 a un amigo o conocido al final de una comida, cuando quieres compartir el gasto en vez de que te inviten. 나누다 significa dividir, y -아/어서 lo une a 내다, pagar; con 우리 (nosotros), el presente en 해요체 es una propuesta. La respuesta habitual es 그래요 o 좋아요, y a menudo uno paga y el otro le manda su parte."@es ,
                        "Se dit au style poli 해요체 à un ami ou une connaissance à la fin d'un repas, quand on veut partager les frais plutôt qu'être invité. 나누다 veut dire diviser, et -아/어서 le relie à 내다, payer ; avec 우리 (nous), le présent au 해요체 devient une proposition. La réponse habituelle est 그래요 ou 좋아요, et souvent l'un paie, puis l'autre lui envoie sa part."@fr ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-paying-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-paying-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-paying-3a-d3> .

<#q-paying-3a-d1>
