# Chapter 5: At the market.
<#ch-market>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    dcterms:title "At the market"@en ,
                  "På marknaden"@sv ,
                  "Auf dem Markt"@de ,
                  "En el mercado"@es ,
                  "Au marché"@fr ,
                  "시장에서"@ko ;
    dcterms:description "Alex and Minji shop at a traditional market for a meal they will cook together: asking the price per basket and per item, buying by count, weight and amount, tasting first, and thanking a vendor who throws in extra. Ends with a review of the chapter."@en ,
                        "Alex och Minji handlar på en traditionell marknad inför en måltid de ska laga tillsammans: fråga vad en korg och en sak kostar, köpa efter antal, vikt och summa, smaka först och tacka en försäljare som lägger i lite extra. Avslutas med en repetition av kapitlet."@sv ,
                        "Alex und Minji kaufen auf einem traditionellen Markt für ein Essen ein, das sie zusammen kochen wollen: nach dem Preis pro Korb und pro Stück fragen, nach Stückzahl, Gewicht und Betrag kaufen, erst probieren und einem Verkäufer danken, der etwas dazulegt. Endet mit einer Wiederholung des Kapitels."@de ,
                        "Alex y Minji hacen la compra en un mercado tradicional para una comida que van a cocinar juntos: preguntar el precio por cesta y por pieza, comprar por número, peso e importe, probar primero y dar las gracias a un vendedor que añade algo de regalo. Termina con un repaso del capítulo."@es ,
                        "Alex et Minji font les courses dans un marché traditionnel pour un repas à préparer ensemble : demander le prix au panier et à la pièce, acheter au nombre, au poids et au montant, goûter d'abord et remercier un marchand qui ajoute un petit extra. Se termine par une révision du chapitre."@fr ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-market-r01> ,
                              <#q-market-r02> .

<#ch-market-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-market> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Baskets and tastes

*The next Saturday morning, a covered market near Mangwon station. Alex and Minji are shopping for an afternoon of cooking at Alex's place: 떡볶이, made mild this time, the dish Minji saved for another day when they chose dinner, and 김치전, kimchi pancakes.*

**민지:** 양파부터 사요. 저 채소 가게가 싸요.\\
*Let's start with onions. That vegetable stall is cheap.*

**알렉스:** 사장님, 이 양파 한 바구니에 얼마예요?\\
*Excuse me, how much is a basket of these onions?*

**사장님:** 삼천 원이요.\\
*Three thousand won.*

**알렉스:** 그럼 한 바구니 주세요.\\
*One basket, then, please.*

**사장님:** 네, 봉투에 담아 드릴게요.\\
*Sure, I'll put them in a bag for you.*

*At the fruit stall next door, Minji stops at a box of mandarins.*

**민지:** 사장님, 이 귤 맛 좀 봐도 돼요?\\
*Excuse me, may I taste one of these mandarins?*

**사장님:** 그럼요, 한번 드셔 보세요.\\
*Of course, go on, try some.*

**민지:** 와, 달아요! 알렉스 씨도 먹어 봐요.\\
*Wow, it's sweet! You try one too, Alex.*

---

**한 바구니에 얼마예요?**: "How much is a basket?" At a market, onions, potatoes, peppers and fruit often sit in small plastic baskets, and the basket is what you buy. 바구니 means basket, and 에 after a quantity means "per"; the next step shows more of it. 이거 얼마예요?, which you know from Everyday Korean phrases, asks about one thing; 한 바구니에 asks the price of the unit. The vendor answers with just the amount and -요: 삼천 원이요.

**맛 좀 봐도 돼요?**: "May I taste it?" 맛을 보다, literally "to see the taste", means to taste. 좀 softens the question, and -아/어도 돼요? asks permission, as in 먹어도 돼요? (may I eat this?). Ask it before you take a piece from a stall.

**그럼요**: "Of course." Minji said it on the evening they met, when Alex asked whether she knew a good place to eat.

**한번 드셔 보세요**: "Go on, try some." -아/어 보다 means to try doing something: 먹어 보다 is to try eating. 드셔 보세요 is 드시다, the honorific for eating that you know from the café, with -어 보세요, so the vendor honors the customer. 한번, once, makes it an easy offer. Minji's 먹어 봐요 is the same -아/어 보다 with the plain verb 먹다 and no -시-, as between the two friends.

**달아요**: "It's sweet." 달다 means sweet. Alex and Minji call the vendors 사장님, as they called the owner of the 찌개 restaurant."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-market-1a> ,
                         <#q-market-1b> .

<#q-market-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-28T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "한 바구니에 얼마예요?"@ko ;
    solid-memo:back "How much is a basket?"@en ,
                    "Vad kostar en korg?"@sv ,
                    "Was kostet ein Korb?"@de ,
                    "¿Cuánto cuesta una cesta?"@es ,
                    "Combien coûte un panier ?"@fr ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Asked by a customer at a market stall where vegetables or fruit are sold in small baskets, in polite 해요체. 바구니 is a basket, and 에 after a quantity means per, so it asks the price of one basket. The vendor answers with the price, such as 삼천 원이요, and you say 한 바구니 주세요."@en ,
                        "Frågas av en kund vid ett marknadsstånd där grönsaker eller frukt säljs i små korgar, i artig 해요체. 바구니 är en korg, och 에 efter en mängd betyder per, så frågan gäller priset för en korg. Försäljaren svarar med priset, till exempel 삼천 원이요, och man säger 한 바구니 주세요."@sv ,
                        "Fragt ein Kunde an einem Marktstand, an dem Gemüse oder Obst in kleinen Körben verkauft wird, im höflichen 해요체. 바구니 ist ein Korb, und 에 nach einer Menge heißt pro, man fragt also nach dem Preis für einen Korb. Der Verkäufer nennt den Preis, etwa 삼천 원이요, und man sagt 한 바구니 주세요."@de ,
                        "Lo pregunta un cliente en un puesto del mercado donde la verdura o la fruta se vende en cestitas, en el estilo cortés 해요체. 바구니 es una cesta, y 에 tras una cantidad significa por, así que pregunta el precio de una cesta. El vendedor responde con el precio, como 삼천 원이요, y se dice 한 바구니 주세요."@es ,
                        "Demandé par un client à un étal du marché où les légumes ou les fruits se vendent dans de petits paniers, au style poli 해요체. 바구니 est un panier, et 에 après une quantité veut dire par : on demande donc le prix d'un panier. Le marchand répond par le prix, comme 삼천 원이요, et on dit 한 바구니 주세요."@fr ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-market-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-market-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-market-1a-d3> .

