<#ch-market-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-market> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## A little extra

*Another way it goes: on the way out, Alex can't stop thinking about the sweet mandarins and goes back to the fruit stall.*

**알렉스:** 사장님, 이 귤 한 봉지 주세요.\\
*A bag of these mandarins, please.*

**사장님:** 한 봉지에 오천 원이에요. 하나 더 넣어 드렸어요.\\
*Five thousand won a bag. I put in an extra one for you.*

**알렉스:** 와, 감사합니다! 많이 파세요.\\
*Wow, thank you! Hope business goes well.*

**사장님:** 감사합니다, 또 오세요!\\
*Thank you, come again!*

**민지:** 알렉스 씨, 벌써 단골 같아요!\\
*Alex, you're like a regular already!*

**하나 더 넣어 드렸어요**: "I put in an extra one for you." 하나 더 is one more, and 넣다 is to put in. -아/어 드리다, with the humble 드리다 you know from the café, marks something done for the customer.

**많이 파세요**: "Hope business goes well!", literally "sell a lot". 파세요 is 팔다 (to sell) with -세요: the ㄹ drops before it, and -시- honors the vendor. It is the customer's friendly goodbye after buying, and the vendor answers 감사합니다 or 또 오세요 (come again).

---

**Culture note:** Traditional markets sell by the basket, the bag or the bunch as well as by weight, and many vendors put in a little extra for free, called **덤**, more so for a regular customer, a **단골**. Asking for a small discount with 좀 깎아 주세요, which you know from Everyday Korean phrases, can work at a market stall, but not in a supermarket. Tasting before you buy is common at fruit stalls, and vendors often offer it themselves.

Customers call a vendor 사장님, or, for a woman of about their parents' generation, often 이모 (literally a mother's sister). Cash and bank transfers are welcome, many stalls take cards, and most traditional markets take Onnuri gift certificates (온누리상품권), vouchers that can be spent only at registered stalls and shops in traditional markets and designated shopping streets. A stall usually puts your shopping in a plastic bag, and you can ask for one with 봉투 주세요, from Everyday Korean phrases. Large supermarkets may not give out single-use plastic bags at all, so people bring their own or buy a paper bag or a pay-as-you-throw trash bag (종량제 봉투) at the checkout."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-market-3a> ,
                         <#q-market-3b> .

<#q-market-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-28T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "많이 파세요"@ko ;
    solid-memo:back "Hope business goes well!"@en ,
                    "Lycka till med försäljningen!"@sv ,
                    "Gute Geschäfte noch!"@de ,
                    "¡Que venda mucho!"@es ,
                    "Bonnes ventes !"@fr ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Said by a customer to a market vendor when leaving, in polite 해요체 with -시- honoring the vendor; literally \"sell a lot\", from 팔다, to sell. It is a friendly goodbye after buying, often after 감사합니다. The vendor answers 감사합니다, 또 오세요."@en ,
                        "Sägs av en kund till en försäljare på marknaden när man går, i artig 해요체 med -시- som hedrar försäljaren; ordagrant ”sälj mycket”, av 팔다, sälja. Det är ett vänligt farväl efter ett köp, ofta efter 감사합니다. Försäljaren svarar 감사합니다, 또 오세요."@sv ,
                        "Sagt ein Kunde beim Gehen zu einem Marktverkäufer, im höflichen 해요체 mit -시-, das den Verkäufer ehrt; wörtlich „verkaufen Sie viel“, von 팔다, verkaufen. Ein freundlicher Abschied nach dem Einkauf, oft nach 감사합니다. Der Verkäufer antwortet 감사합니다, 또 오세요."@de ,
                        "Lo dice un cliente a un vendedor del mercado al marcharse, en el estilo cortés 해요체 con -시-, que honra al vendedor; literalmente «venda mucho», de 팔다, vender. Es una despedida amable tras comprar, a menudo después de 감사합니다. El vendedor responde 감사합니다, 또 오세요."@es ,
                        "Dit par un client à un marchand en partant, au style poli 해요체 avec -시-, qui honore le marchand ; littéralement « vendez beaucoup », de 팔다, vendre. C'est un au revoir aimable après un achat, souvent après 감사합니다. Le marchand répond 감사합니다, 또 오세요."@fr ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-market-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-market-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-market-3a-d3> .

