<#ch-family-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-family> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Words for elders

Korean honors older people not only with -시- but with words of their own. You use them for an elder's meal, sleep, age or home, and the plain words for everyone else.

- **드시다** for 먹다 (to eat), which you know from the café. 잡수시다 is even more deferential, heard mostly to and about the very old.
- **진지** for 밥 (rice, a meal), but only for an elder's meal: 진지 드세요 (please eat), 진지 드셨어요? (have you eaten?).
- **계시다** for 있다 when a person is somewhere: 할머니는 방에 계세요 (Grandma is in her room). 안녕히 계세요, from Basic Korean, is the same word.
- **주무시다** for 자다 (to sleep), as in 안녕히 주무세요 (good night), which you know from Everyday Korean phrases.

*Dinner is on the table, and Minji's grandmother comes out of her room.*

**민지:** 할머니, 진지 드세요.\\
*Grandma, dinner's ready. Please eat.*

**할머니:** 그래, 다 같이 먹자.\\
*All right, let's all eat.*

**알렉스:** 할머니, 실례지만 연세가 어떻게 되세요?\\
*Grandma, if you don't mind me asking, how old are you?*

**할머니:** 올해 여든둘이에요.\\
*Eighty-two this year.*

**알렉스:** 와, 정말 정정하시네요!\\
*Wow, you're so full of life!*

---

- **연세** for 나이 (age): 연세가 어떻게 되세요? To someone your own age you ask 몇 년생이에요?, as at the café, and to a child 몇 살이야?
- **댁** for 집 (home): 할머니 댁 (Grandma's house), 댁이 어디세요? (where do you live?).
- **말씀** for 말 (words), as in 말씀 많이 들었습니다.
- **성함** for 이름 (name), as in 성함이 어떻게 되세요?, from Everyday Korean phrases.

Two words work the other way and lower yourself instead: **드리다** for 주다 when you give something to an elder, and **뵙다** for 보다 or 만나다 when you see one, as in 처음 뵙겠습니다. **정정하다** praises an old person for being hale and hearty, and is said only of the old.

**Honor the person, not the listener.** These words and -시- go with the person you speak about, while the ending, with 요 or without, goes with the person you speak to. So after dinner Alex asks Minji in 반말 about her grandmother and still says 주무시다:

**알렉스:** 할머니는 벌써 주무셔?\\
*Is your grandma asleep already?*

**민지:** 응, 일찍 주무셔.\\
*Yeah, she goes to bed early.*

**할머니는 벌써 주무셔?** has no 요, for Minji, but 주무시다, for her grandmother. Nobody uses these words about themselves: Alex says 저는 많이 먹었어요, never 진지 or 드셨어요, and the grandmother says 먹자 about her own meal."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-family-2a> ,
                         <#q-family-2b> ,
                         <#q-family-2c> .

<#q-family-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-28T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "할머니, 진지 드세요"@ko ;
    solid-memo:back "Please have your meal, Grandma (to an elder)"@en ,
                    "Var så god och ät, farmor (till en äldre)"@sv ,
                    "Bitte essen Sie, Großmutter (zu einer älteren Person)"@de ,
                    "Coma, abuela, por favor (a una persona mayor)"@es ,
                    "Mangez, grand-mère, je vous en prie (à une personne âgée)"@fr ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Said by a grandchild or another younger person to a grandmother or an elderly woman, in polite 해요체 with honorific words, often to call her to the table. 진지 is the honorific word for 밥 (rice, a meal), used only for an elder's meal, and 드세요 is 드시다, the honorific of 먹다. To a friend it would be 밥 먹어. The elder may answer 그래, 먹자 or 그래, 같이 먹자."@en ,
                        "Sägs av ett barnbarn eller någon annan yngre till en farmor, mormor eller äldre kvinna, i artig 해요체 med hedrande ord, ofta för att kalla henne till bordet. 진지 är det hedrande ordet för 밥 (ris, en måltid), som bara används om en äldres måltid, och 드세요 är 드시다, den hedrande formen av 먹다. Till en vän skulle det vara 밥 먹어. Den äldre kan svara 그래, 먹자 eller 그래, 같이 먹자."@sv ,
                        "Sagt ein Enkelkind oder ein anderer jüngerer Mensch zu einer Großmutter oder älteren Frau, im höflichen 해요체 mit ehrenden Wörtern, oft um sie zu Tisch zu rufen. 진지 ist das ehrende Wort für 밥 (Reis, eine Mahlzeit), nur für die Mahlzeit älterer Menschen, und 드세요 ist 드시다, die ehrende Form von 먹다. Zu einem Freund hieße es 밥 먹어. Die Ältere antwortet vielleicht 그래, 먹자 oder 그래, 같이 먹자."@de ,
                        "Lo dice un nieto u otra persona más joven a una abuela o a una mujer mayor, en el estilo cortés 해요체 con palabras honoríficas, a menudo para llamarla a la mesa. 진지 es la palabra honorífica para 밥 (arroz, una comida), solo para la comida de una persona mayor, y 드세요 es 드시다, la forma honorífica de 먹다. A un amigo se le diría 밥 먹어. La persona mayor puede responder 그래, 먹자 o 그래, 같이 먹자."@es ,
                        "Dit par un petit-enfant ou une autre personne plus jeune à une grand-mère ou à une femme âgée, au style poli 해요체 avec des mots honorifiques, souvent pour l'appeler à table. 진지 est le mot honorifique pour 밥 (riz, repas), réservé au repas d'une personne âgée, et 드세요 est 드시다, la forme honorifique de 먹다. À un ami, on dirait 밥 먹어. La personne âgée peut répondre 그래, 먹자 ou 그래, 같이 먹자."@fr ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-family-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-family-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-family-2a-d3> .

<#q-family-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Please sit down here, Grandma (to an elder)"@en ,
                              "Sätt dig här, farmor (till en äldre)"@sv ,
                              "Setzen Sie sich bitte hierher, Großmutter (zu einer älteren Person)"@de ,
                              "Siéntese aquí, abuela (a una persona mayor)"@es ,
                              "Asseyez-vous ici, grand-mère (à une personne âgée)"@fr ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That would be 할머니, 여기 앉으세요; 드세요 here is 드시다, to eat, and 진지 is her meal."@en ,
                              "Det vore 할머니, 여기 앉으세요; 드세요 är här 드시다, att äta, och 진지 är hennes måltid."@sv ,
                              "Das wäre 할머니, 여기 앉으세요; 드세요 ist hier 드시다, essen, und 진지 ist ihre Mahlzeit."@de ,
                              "Eso sería 할머니, 여기 앉으세요; 드세요 aquí es 드시다, comer, y 진지 es su comida."@es ,
                              "Ce serait 할머니, 여기 앉으세요 ; 드세요 est ici 드시다, manger, et 진지 est son repas."@fr .

<#q-family-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Have you eaten, Grandma? (to an elder)"@en ,
                              "Har du ätit, farmor? (till en äldre)"@sv ,
                              "Haben Sie schon gegessen, Großmutter? (zu einer älteren Person)"@de ,
                              "¿Ha comido ya, abuela? (a una persona mayor)"@es ,
                              "Vous avez mangé, grand-mère ? (à une personne âgée)"@fr ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That would be 할머니, 진지 드셨어요?, the elder's form of 밥 먹었어요?; 드세요 asks her to eat now."@en ,
                              "Det vore 할머니, 진지 드셨어요?, den äldres form av 밥 먹었어요?; 드세요 ber henne äta nu."@sv ,
                              "Das wäre 할머니, 진지 드셨어요?, die Form von 밥 먹었어요? für Ältere; 드세요 bittet sie, jetzt zu essen."@de ,
                              "Eso sería 할머니, 진지 드셨어요?, la forma de 밥 먹었어요? para los mayores; 드세요 le pide que coma ahora."@es ,
                              "Ce serait 할머니, 진지 드셨어요?, la forme de 밥 먹었어요? pour les aînés ; 드세요 lui demande de manger maintenant."@fr .

<#q-family-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Come and eat (to a close friend)"@en ,
                              "Kom och ät (till en nära vän)"@sv ,
                              "Komm essen (unter engen Freunden)"@de ,
                              "Ven a comer (a un amigo cercano)"@es ,
                              "Viens manger (à un ami proche)"@fr ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That would be 밥 먹어; 진지 and 드시다 are for elders, never for a friend."@en ,
                              "Det vore 밥 먹어; 진지 och 드시다 används till äldre, aldrig till en vän."@sv ,
                              "Das wäre 밥 먹어; 진지 und 드시다 gelten Älteren, nie einem Freund."@de ,
                              "Eso sería 밥 먹어; 진지 y 드시다 son para los mayores, nunca para un amigo."@es ,
                              "Ce serait 밥 먹어 ; 진지 et 드시다 s'emploient pour les aînés, jamais pour un ami."@fr .

<#q-family-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-28T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "연세가 어떻게 되세요?"@ko ;
    solid-memo:back "May I ask how old you are? (to an elder)"@en ,
                    "Får jag fråga hur gammal du är? (till en äldre)"@sv ,
                    "Darf ich fragen, wie alt Sie sind? (zu einer älteren Person)"@de ,
                    "¿Puedo preguntarle su edad? (a una persona mayor)"@es ,
                    "Puis-je vous demander votre âge ? (à une personne âgée)"@fr ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Asked of an older person, such as a friend's grandparent, in polite 해요체 with -시-. 연세 is the honorific word for 나이 (age); to someone of your own generation you ask 몇 년생이에요?, and to a child 몇 살이야? The answer is the age, as in 올해 여든둘이에요 (eighty-two this year)."@en ,
                        "Frågas till en äldre person, till exempel en väns mor- eller farförälder, i artig 해요체 med -시-. 연세 är det hedrande ordet för 나이 (ålder); till någon i ens egen generation frågar man 몇 년생이에요?, och till ett barn 몇 살이야? Svaret är åldern, som i 올해 여든둘이에요 (åttiotvå i år)."@sv ,
                        "Gefragt an einen älteren Menschen, etwa die Großeltern eines Freundes, im höflichen 해요체 mit -시-. 연세 ist das ehrende Wort für 나이 (Alter); jemanden der eigenen Generation fragt man 몇 년생이에요?, ein Kind 몇 살이야? Die Antwort ist das Alter, etwa 올해 여든둘이에요 (dieses Jahr zweiundachtzig)."@de ,
                        "Se pregunta a una persona mayor, como el abuelo o la abuela de un amigo, en el estilo cortés 해요체 con -시-. 연세 es la palabra honorífica para 나이 (edad); a alguien de tu generación se le pregunta 몇 년생이에요?, y a un niño, 몇 살이야? La respuesta es la edad, como en 올해 여든둘이에요 (ochenta y dos este año)."@es ,
                        "Se demande à une personne âgée, par exemple un grand-parent d'un ami, au style poli 해요체 avec -시-. 연세 est le mot honorifique pour 나이 (âge) ; à quelqu'un de sa génération, on demande 몇 년생이에요?, et à un enfant 몇 살이야? La réponse est l'âge, comme 올해 여든둘이에요 (quatre-vingt-deux ans cette année)."@fr ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-family-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-family-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-family-2b-d3> .

