<#ch-regular-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-regular> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## One question, three levels

**The level follows the listener.** Korean has no single way to ask "have you eaten?": you choose by the person you ask.

- **밥 먹었어?** to a close friend, a younger sibling or a child: 반말.
- **밥 먹었어요?** to an acquaintance, a peer you are polite with, or the owner: 해요체, and the course's title.
- **식사하셨어요?** to a senior at work or an older acquaintance: 식사 is the formal word for a meal, 식사하다 is to have one, and -시- honors the person who eats, here the senior you ask.
- **진지 드셨어요?** to grandparents and other elders, with 진지 and 드시다 from dinner with Minji's family.

An offer to pay climbs the same way: **내가 살게** to Minji, **제가 살게요** to an acquaintance, as Minji said when she paid for their first dinner, and **제가 사 드리겠습니다** in the rare case a junior treats a senior, with humble 드리다 and 합쇼체.

**I and me.** 저 and 제가 are the humble words for I; in 반말 they become 나 and 내가. The same goes for 저도 and 나도 (me too).

---

*Back at the table, Alex's phone buzzes. The screen says 김 팀장님.*

**알렉스:** 네, 팀장님. … 아, 월요일 아침 아홉 시요? 알겠습니다.\\
*Yes, hello. … Oh, nine on Monday morning? Understood.*

**알렉스:** 아니요, 괜찮습니다. 지금 밖에서 식사하고 있어요. 팀장님은 식사하셨어요?\\
*No, that's fine. I'm out having dinner. Have you eaten?*

**알렉스:** 네, 월요일에 뵙겠습니다. 들어가세요.\\
*Yes, see you on Monday. Goodbye.*

**민지:** 누구야?\\
*Who was that?*

**알렉스:** 팀장님. 월요일 회의 때문에 전화하셨어. 미안, 오늘은 내가 살게.\\
*My team leader, about Monday's meeting. Sorry about that. It's my treat today.*

**민지:** 진짜? 그럼 커피는 내가 살게.\\
*Really? Then the coffee's on me.*

**알겠습니다**, **괜찮습니다** and **뵙겠습니다** are 합쇼체 set lines of work, as at the team dinner; 뵙다 is the humble word for seeing someone senior, as in 처음 뵙겠습니다 from Everyday Korean phrases. On the phone, **들어가세요** (literally "go in", as in the team leader's 조심히 들어가요) is a polite goodbye, and with -시- it is fine to a senior. The rest Alex says in honorific 해요체, and about Alex's own dinner without -시-: 식사하고 있어요.

**전화하셨어**: 반말, because Alex is talking to Minji, but with -시-, because the team leader is the one who called. As with 할머니는 벌써 주무셔? at dinner with Minji's family, -시- honors the person you talk about.

**반말 stays where it was agreed.** However friendly the owner or the team leader becomes, Alex's 반말 is for Minji only. **오늘은 내가 살게** and **커피는 내가 살게** are the treats of their first dinner, now between friends."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-regular-2a> ,
                         <#q-regular-2b> .

<#q-regular-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-28T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "식사하셨어요?"@ko ;
    solid-memo:back "Have you eaten? (to someone senior)"@en ,
                    "Har du ätit? (till någon äldre eller överordnad)"@sv ,
                    "Haben Sie schon gegessen? (zu Älteren oder Vorgesetzten)"@de ,
                    "¿Ya ha comido? (a un mayor o superior)"@es ,
                    "Vous avez déjà mangé ? (à un aîné ou un supérieur)"@fr ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Asked in honorific 해요체 of a senior at work, an older acquaintance or a customer: 식사 is the formal word for a meal, 식사하다 is to have one, and -시- honors the person who eats, here the listener. Around mealtimes it is also a greeting, much like \"how are you?\". You answer about yourself without -시-: 네, 먹었어요 or 아직요. To a close friend it is 밥 먹었어?, and to grandparents 진지 드셨어요?"@en ,
                        "Frågas i hedrande 해요체 till en överordnad på jobbet, en äldre bekant eller en kund: 식사 är det formella ordet för måltid, 식사하다 att äta en måltid, och -시- hedrar den som äter, här den man frågar. Kring måltiderna är det också en hälsning, ungefär som ”hur mår du?”. Man svarar om sig själv utan -시-: 네, 먹었어요 eller 아직요. Till en nära vän säger man 밥 먹었어?, och till far- och morföräldrar 진지 드셨어요?"@sv ,
                        "Gefragt im ehrenden 해요체, an Vorgesetzte, ältere Bekannte oder Kunden: 식사 ist das gehobene Wort für eine Mahlzeit, 식사하다 heißt essen, und -시- ehrt die Person, die isst, hier die angesprochene. Um die Essenszeiten ist es auch ein Gruß, etwa wie „wie geht's?“. Man antwortet über sich selbst ohne -시-: 네, 먹었어요 oder 아직요. Unter engen Freunden heißt es 밥 먹었어?, zu den Großeltern 진지 드셨어요?"@de ,
                        "Se pregunta en 해요체 honorífico a un superior en el trabajo, a un conocido mayor o a un cliente: 식사 es la palabra formal para comida, 식사하다 es comer, y -시- honra a quien come, aquí la persona a quien se pregunta. A la hora de comer también es un saludo, algo así como «¿qué tal?». Se responde sobre uno mismo sin -시-: 네, 먹었어요 o 아직요. A un amigo cercano se le dice 밥 먹었어?, y a los abuelos 진지 드셨어요?"@es ,
                        "Se demande au 해요체 honorifique à un supérieur au travail, à une connaissance plus âgée ou à un client : 식사 est le mot soutenu pour repas, 식사하다 veut dire prendre un repas, et -시- honore celui qui mange, ici la personne à qui l'on parle. Autour des repas, c'est aussi une salutation, un peu comme « ça va ? ». On répond sur soi-même sans -시- : 네, 먹었어요 ou 아직요. À un ami proche, on dit 밥 먹었어?, et aux grands-parents 진지 드셨어요?"@fr ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-regular-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-regular-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-regular-2a-d3> .

